  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Given that ${\\lambda }_{1}=6-\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}$ are eigenvalues of a real $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$, find $3({\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}).$",
  "worked_solution": "Since ${\\lambda }_{1}=6-\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{\u2015}{\\lambda }}_{1}=6+\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}) & =3(6-\\text{i}+(6+\\text{i})) \\\\  & =3(12) \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
